parts.conclusion:
5.

Conclusions

From our findings, we reached the following conclusions:

Images from Landsat 8 and Landsat 9, taken on different dates and in different colors of light, map local climate zones (types of neighborhoods) well and accurately, and show how these zones are linked to land surface temperature from the countryside into the city;

We can adjust land surface temperatures by combining satellite data with other data. This gets around the problem that the satellites pass over only every 16 days and at a fixed time of day. It lets us watch and study how neighborhood types and surface temperatures are linked at the hottest times of day;

Adjusted surface temperatures let us track and understand how heat stress differs between neighborhood types and from the countryside into the city;

By laying heat stress maps on top of neighborhood maps, we can link heat stress to each type of neighborhood and measure how much each one adds to heat or helps reduce it. From this, we concluded that rural areas, areas with plants (forests and low plants) and wetlands are important for reducing heat and keeping conditions more comfortable from the countryside into the city;

Extreme heat stress should be watched closely in densely built-up areas, so that warnings can be issued and action taken on very hot days. Some parts of densely built-up areas can also feel heat stress even on a hot day in the cold season;

An early warning system can be built from the methods we used in this study, to alert people about heat stress in different neighborhood types.

Overall, our study improves understanding of how different neighborhood types respond to heat stress when temperatures become extreme, from the countryside into the city. We recommend checking the physical comfort conditions we measured against the real-life experiences of the communities in the area. Ways of studying the area together with local people, including learning how they see and experience heat and cold, will help confirm results based on physical measurements.

Our findings show patterns across eThekwini, so similar studies are needed in other places, because our results cannot be applied to the whole country. Future studies could benefit from installing detailed instruments that measure how comfortable it feels, spread fairly across the country. These would capture conditions in different parts of the country and also help improve satellite data.

Policies and plans for adding green spaces need to be expanded and promoted in the municipality, because neighborhood types with plants are very good at reducing heat. Future studies can also look at how changes in neighborhood types will affect how comfortable it feels in the future.
